  "text": {
    "intro": "The Pinhole Amateur Astronomical Society has one good telescope and a great many strong opinions about who may point it where.\nFive astronomers are involved. Determine each astronomer's day, target and mishap.\n- **Astronomer**: Cornelius, Niamh, Sunniva, Dmitri and Ptolemy.\n- **Day** (listed from earlier to later): Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday.\n- **Observed**: a lunar occultation, Saturn's rings, a comet fragment, the Andromeda galaxy and the Crab Nebula.\n- **Mishap**: a flat battery, a cracked filter, a dropped lens cap, a dewed-up eyepiece and an unexpected cloud bank.\nEvery astronomer has a different day, target and mishap, so within each category every item is used exactly once.",
    "clues": [
      "The astronomer who is undone by a dewed-up eyepiece does not study the Andromeda galaxy. The astronomer who studies the Andromeda galaxy observes on Tuesday.",
      "The astronomer who is undone by a flat battery is earlier than Tuesday in day order.",
      "The astronomer who studies a comet fragment and Tuesday are exactly one day apart in day order, in one direction or the other.",
      "Niamh is exactly one day earlier than Tuesday, with no day in between.",
      "The astronomer who studies a lunar occultation is later than Thursday in day order.",
      "Thursday falls somewhere between the astronomer who is undone by a cracked filter and the astronomer who is undone by a dropped lens cap in day order.",
      "Ptolemy and the astronomer who is undone by a dropped lens cap are exactly two days apart in day order.",
      "The astronomer who is undone by a dropped lens cap falls somewhere between the astronomer who studies a lunar occultation and the astronomer who studies the Crab Nebula in day order.",
      "Cornelius and the astronomer who studies a comet fragment are exactly one day apart in day order, in one direction or the other.",
      "Dmitri and the astronomer who is undone by a cracked filter are exactly one day apart in day order, in one direction or the other."
    ]
  }
